ome to work.\n\nAnswer: \boxed{D}
2025-12-24 01:33:49 | INFO | Q228 id=DvkYRhu-TP0_00-01-59_00-02-29 | baseline_pred=D | gold=D | prompt_variant=low | followup_pred=D | followup_correct=True | raw=The audio contains a heated exchange in what appears to be a courtroom setting. A lawyer points to someone and asks if they are surprised to learn that person is not the defendant. The lawyer then identifies his client, Mr. Staky, who stands up. Immediately after Mr. Staky stands up, someone shouts "Objection." This objection is followed by the lawyer stating that the person the first speaker identified is Hollis Early, a bartender, and that he has an alibi. Then, another person shouts "Objection" followed by "Mr. Goodman, really?" The judge then intervenes, saying, "All right, settle down everybody, settle, settle."\n\nThe question asks who shouted "objection." There are at least two instances of "Objection" being shouted.\n\n1. The first "Objection" is shouted immediately after the defense lawyer points out his client, Mr. Staky, who stands up. In a courtroom, objections are typically raised by the opposing counsel (prosecution or defense) or sometimes by the judge if a procedural rule is violated, though usually, the judge rules on objections, not raises them in this manner unless they are self-correcting or addressing a clear issue. Given the context of the lawyer presenting his client, the objection is likely from the opposing side, which would be the prosecution if the first speaker is the defense, or vice versa.\n\n2. The second "Objection" is shouted by someone who is then addressed as "Mr. Goodman," and the judge responds to this person. The judge then says, "Mr. Goodman, really? You didn't recognize him either, your honor." This suggests Mr. Goodman is likely one of the attorneys or perhaps a party involved, and the judge is addressing him.\n\nThe initial exchange involves a lawyer (likely defense, as he is pointing out his client) making a statement, and then an immediate objection. The lawyer t